  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Phishing for Information", "technique": "T1598", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-12 09:14:03 CEST, Suricata logged the first of a sustained burst of 'ET SCAN SMTP VRFY command attempt' alerts (signature_id 2002992) with data.srcip=198.51.100.42 → data.dstip=10.108.57.10 on data.dstport=25. The source issued SMTP VRFY commands against a curated wordlist of likely mailbox names — generic role accounts (admin, administrator, postmaster, root, webmaster, helpdesk, security) plus department roles (ceo, cfo, cto, finance, hr) plus plausible personal-name accounts (john.smith, alice.jones, david.miller, laura.taylor). VRFY asks the mail server to confirm whether a local mailbox exists; a 250/252 response confirms a valid recipient, a 550 confirms it does not. The probe used polite ~8-second inter-command spacing — slow enough to slip under naive 'X connections per second' rate thresholds, but the curated wordlist + sequential single-source pattern is the textbook SMTP user-enumeration fingerprint.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET SCAN SMTP VRFY command attempt' (sig_id 2002992) fired repeatedly with the same data.srcip=198.51.100.42 against data.dstip=10.108.57.10 data.dstport=25 within a tight window. Univariate signature: single external source issuing >10 SMTP VRFY commands against one mail server inside 30 minutes.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:14:03+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Phishing for Information", "technique": "T1589.002", "what_happened": "At 09:28:30 CEST (~2-minute pause after the VRFY phase ended at ~09:26), the same data.srcip=198.51.100.42 switched enumeration method: instead of VRFY it began issuing 'MAIL FROM:<probe@external.test>' followed by 'RCPT TO:<user@corp.local>' against the same mail server 10.108.57.10:25. RCPT TO enumeration is the fallback when VRFY is disabled — the server's accept (250) vs reject (550 'no such user') on the RCPT TO line leaks the same valid-mailbox information. Suricata fired 'ET SCAN Rcpt To command flood' style SMTP enumeration alerts. The RCPT TO phase reused the high-value subset of the wordlist (admin, administrator, postmaster, root, webmaster, john.smith, alice.jones, david.miller) — the operator filtered the wordlist down to the names most worth re-confirming, a methodical-operator workflow signature.", "chain_reason": "Suricata SMTP enumeration signatures with data.srcip=198.51.100.42 → data.dstip=10.108.57.10 data.dstport=25, the RCPT TO command pattern with a spoofed MAIL FROM (<probe@external.test>) against a curated recipient list. The ~2-minute method switch from VRFY to RCPT TO against the same target is the operator-adapts-when-VRFY-yields-little signature.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:28:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Cumulative Volume", "technique": "T1598", "what_happened": "Across the 09:14-09:42 window (~28 minutes), Suricata aggregated ~31 distinct SMTP-enumeration alerts with data.srcip=198.51.100.42 — combining the VRFY phase (Phase 1) and the RCPT TO phase (Phase 2) against mail server 10.108.57.10:25. The cumulative volume + single-external-source + curated-wordlist + two-method (VRFY then RCPT TO) signature is the canonical SMTP user-enumeration fingerprint that MITRE T1598 (Phishing for Information) and T1589.002 (Gather Victim Identity Information: Email Addresses) describe. SMTP user enumeration is not an attack in itself — it is the reconnaissance step that builds the validated recipient list for a later spearphishing or credential-spray campaign.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh correlation rule 'SMTP user enumeration from single external source' (rule.groups containing 'suricata' + 'ids') fired on aggregate count of SMTP VRFY/EXPN/RCPT TO enumeration signatures from the same data.srcip within a 1-hour window. The MITRE mapping T1598 was attached automatically by the rule.", "first_seen": "2026-05-12T09:41:55+0200"}
